<!--
  GRA-64 emulator finding: LeakCanary decides "this process is an
  instrumented test, not a real app" by checking whether
  `leak_canary_test_class_name` (org.junit.Test, by default) is on the
  classpath — a heuristic square/leakcanary#1968 documents as a known false
  positive for exactly this app's own shape: MockWebServer's
  `okhttp3.mockwebserver.MockWebServer` extends
  `org.junit.rules.ExternalResource`, which keeps junit:junit a real,
  resolvable-at-runtime dependency of this debug build (see
  sample/build.gradle.kts's own comment on why it cannot simply be
  excluded), and LeakCanary reads that presence as a test run and disables
  itself entirely — silently: `setup`'s `leakcanary` row still reports
  hooked, because the disablement happens deeper, inside LeakCanary's own
  heap-dump trigger, not at the point this project's hook attaches.
  square/leakcanary#2048 added this exact override for the exact case named
  in its own doc: "when apps may ship JUnit in their debug classpaths"
  (OkHttp's MockWebServer, by name). Pointed at a class this app's own
  runtime classpath never has — LeakCanary's probe is `Class.forName`,
  which simply never finds it, so the "am I in a test" check never fires
  true.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="leak_canary_test_class_name" translatable="false">live.gravitylabs.porthole.LeakCanaryNeverPresentInThisApp</string>
</resources>
